+<main class="subpage-main"><section class="subpage-hero container"><span class="kicker">PLANEBYTE POLICIES</span><h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p>Effective 17 November 2026 · planebyte-creem-global-2026-11-17-v3</p></section><section class="container legal-layout"><article class="legal-copy"><section id="privacy-0"><h2>1. Scope and responsible organization</h2><p>This Policy explains the account, workspace, research, security and payment information processed for PlaneByte Intent Radar and Growth Intelligence, and related customer support. PlaneByte is operated by <strong>Planebyte, an individually operated business owned by Piyush Agrawal</strong>, with its legal-notice/business address at <strong>House No. 1005, Attabira, Bargarh, Odisha 768027, India</strong>. Support, privacy requests and grievances may be sent to contact@planebyte.com. Our business website is https://planebyte.com and the application is https://app.planebyte.com.</p><p>Where an organization controls information uploaded to its workspace, it may determine the purposes and permitted users of that information. PlaneByte operates the service on its behalf and also processes account administration, service security and transaction records for the purposes described here. A separate applicable data-processing agreement or mandatory law may specify additional responsibilities. A workspace member&#x27;s personal rights and a workspace owner&#x27;s authority are not interchangeable.</p><p>This Policy does not authorize an unconnected third-party integration, unpublished product, outreach campaign or provider access. Third-party websites, Google and Creem also provide their own applicable privacy notices. Marketing-site analytics or nonessential tracking, if introduced, require an accurate inventory and separate notice/consent where required; this draft does not assert that every marketing-site script has been audited.</p></section><section id="privacy-1"><h2>2. Information we process</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto"><table><tr><td>Category</td><td>Examples and source</td></tr><tr><td>Account identity</td><td>Company email, name, company name, verified-email status and the sign-in method supplied by you or verified Google claims.</td></tr><tr><td>Authentication and security</td><td>A securely hashed local password when configured; verification/recovery records; session and revocation records; consent-version records; abuse/rate-limit and security events. Google passwords are not collected by PlaneByte.</td></tr><tr><td>Workspace administration</td><td>Workspace/company profile, roles, invitations, membership, onboarding progress, preferences and authorized integration references.</td></tr><tr><td>Research and customer content</td><td>Company websites, reviewed ICPs, offerings, keywords, questions, assistant conversations, source excerpts, URLs, publication/collection dates, saved signals, opportunities, monitors and reports. These come from you, authorized workspace users and permitted sources.</td></tr><tr><td>Usage and diagnostics</td><td>Operation/job states, quota reservations and settlements, reported provider tokens, available cost estimates, errors and bounded operational diagnostics.</td></tr><tr><td>Billing</td><td>Plan and price snapshots, billing contact/address supplied for checkout, currency and total, order and provider references, test/live classification, payment verification, access dates, receipts and refund-review/reconciliation records. Card details are entered only on Creem&#x27;s hosted checkout pages.</td></tr><tr><td>Messages and support</td><td>Transactional delivery identifiers, delivery/bounce/complaint events, suppression information and enquiries you send to support.</td></tr></table></div><p>Verification and recovery credentials are short-lived. Verification values used for comparison are protected with one-way cryptographic checks; message content can temporarily exist in encrypted transactional delivery storage so the requested email can be delivered. Plaintext local passwords are not stored. Do not include payment-card details, passwords, private keys or verification codes in support enquiries or research prompts.</p><p>Subscription records may include provider customer, checkout, subscription, payment, mandate-status and invoice references; verified billing country; plan and trial-conversion choice; consent version/time; renewal/cancellation dates; and authenticated lifecycle events. Raw webhook payloads are encrypted at rest and access is restricted. Payment methods are collected by hosted providers; PlaneByte does not store raw card numbers or CVVs.</p></section><section id="privacy-2"><h2>3. Why information is used</h2><p>We use the information needed to provide the service you request: verify and secure accounts; provision the correct workspace and roles; preserve onboarding progress; run authorized research and AI tasks; save and retrieve results; account for usage; verify payments; activate and expire fixed-term access; reconcile refunds; deliver necessary account/billing messages; handle support; and maintain the service.</p><p>We also use appropriate records to prevent fraud and abuse, investigate incidents and disputes, enforce source and workspace permissions, and meet applicable legal obligations. Where consent is required, we obtain it for the specified purpose and provide the applicable withdrawal route. Where another lawful basis is permitted and appropriate, processing is limited to that basis. Acceptance of general Terms is not treated as blanket consent to unrelated marketing or every future use.</p><p>Research relevance or intent labels are interpretations of available evidence. They are not conclusive decisions about an individual or a guarantee that someone wants to be contacted. Workspace users must review results and ensure their subsequent use is lawful.</p></section><section id="privacy-3"><h2>4. Google, integrations and workspace access</h2><p>Google sign-in supplies the verified identity claims needed to authenticate you. It does not automatically authorize access to Gmail, Google Sheets or a company&#x27;s records. Additional integrations require the permissions and authorization applicable to that connection. Credentials for supported customer connections stay on the server and are subject to workspace ownership checks.</p><p>Workspace roles determine which authorized users can see or manage workspace information. Owners and administrators may manage membership within their authority. An email-domain match does not automatically join an account to an existing workspace. Linking a password and Google identity requires an authenticated process; an email match alone does not authorize merging accounts.</p></section><section id="privacy-4"><h2>5. Research sources and AI processing</h2><p>Authorized research can retrieve public or otherwise permitted material from operational sources. Records can include usernames or author references, professional/company information, quotations, URLs and timestamps where the source provides them and the workflow permits their use. Public availability does not mean unrestricted reuse or erase individuals&#x27; applicable rights. Source coverage is described in the application; unavailable or unapproved connections are not represented as operational.</p><p>When you request an AI feature, the relevant company context, task instructions and source excerpts may be sent to the configured AI provider to produce the requested result. The current primary AI infrastructure is Google Cloud Vertex AI. AI responses can be incomplete or inaccurate. Review suggestions before saving or acting on them. We do not represent a source quote as an AI-verified fact, or claim a provider training/retention opt-out that has not been contractually verified. Provider-specific processing controls must be reflected accurately in the published disclosures and applicable agreements.</p></section><section id="privacy-5"><h2>6. Service providers, disclosures and processing locations</h2><p>The current architecture uses Vercel for the application frontend, Google Cloud for the API/workers and Vertex AI, Neon PostgreSQL for stored application data, Creem for hosted checkout and billing, and Netlify for the separate marketing website. Resend delivers transactional account messages. Creem is merchant of record for new payments; historical financial records retain their original attribution.</p><p>Providers receive information relevant to the function they perform. The application API/workers are deployed in India and the current database region is in the United States; other provider processing depends on the applicable service configuration and agreements. Do not infer that all information remains in one country. Applicable cross-border processing requirements and provider disclosures must be verified before publication.</p><p>We may disclose information to authorized workspace users, necessary service providers, professional advisers handling a relevant matter, or public authorities where disclosure is lawfully required. We may also preserve or disclose appropriate records to investigate security incidents, fraud, disputes or legal claims. Such disclosures must be limited to the relevant purpose and authority. This Policy does not authorize sale of workspace content or unrestricted disclosure to data partners.</p><p>Creem is the sole merchant of record for new checkout in all supported countries, including India where it permits transactions. It handles payment processing, supported currency presentation, applicable taxes, invoices and its hosted billing portal. Creem applies geographic and payment-method restrictions. Provider privacy and payment terms apply to information submitted directly to it. Subscription messages include trial, payment, failure, cancellation and refund notices. A successful checkout is not evidence that merchant payouts have settled.</p></section><section id="privacy-6"><h2>7. Cookies and local preferences</h2><p>The application uses essential session cookies and security mechanisms for authentication and protection of account actions. It may store functional preferences such as theme, navigation state and workspace context. Production authentication cookies are protected against ordinary client-side access and use secure transport settings.</p><p>Disabling essential cookies can prevent sign-in and account functions from working. A theme preference is separate from permission to perform research, access a source, process a payment or send outreach. Any nonessential analytics or advertising technology requires its own accurate disclosure and applicable consent controls; no advertising or analytics consent is bundled into an account verification code.</p></section><section id="privacy-7"><h2>8. Transactional messages and communication choices</h2><p>Account verification, password recovery, explicit team invitations, payment confirmations, renewal/expiry notices and important security messages support the requested service. Delivery is bounded, deduplicated and subject to suppression and authorization checks. A queued message does not establish that it arrived; delivery and complaint events may be processed from the configured email provider.</p><p>General workspace notifications require the relevant enabled feature and recipient preferences. Bulk outreach and marketing are separate from transactional messages and are not authorized merely by enabling account email delivery. An opt-out or suppression record may need to be retained so the choice continues to be honored. The approved deletion process below will explain the scope of such retention.</p></section><section id="privacy-8"><h2>9. Retention after expiry or account closure</h2><p>Fixed-term access expires at its recorded time without automatically deleting saved research, payment history or other retained records. Workspace closure, personal account closure, expiry, membership removal and a verified deletion request have different effects.</p><p>After verified account closure, ordinary account data is scheduled for deletion or anonymization within 30 days, subject to justified exceptions below. Backup removal follows the normal retention cycle, targeted at no more than 90 days after verified closure. These targets come from the owner’s launch policy. The operator records closure and its deadlines; submitting a request alone does not execute a purge. Existing workspace exports and a personal account-detail download are available, and a fuller export can be requested for review.</p><p>Retention must account for the purpose and applicable obligations for each record category. Accounting/refund records, consent and security records, legal holds/disputes, contact/message history and suppression information can require different treatment from an unused research draft. Backups are recovery copies; any eventual deletion process must define how deletions remain effective after recovery and how retained backup copies age out, without promising immediate removal from every copy.</p></section><section id="privacy-9"><h2>10. Requests, correction, withdrawal and deletion</h2><p>Depending on applicable law and your relationship with the workspace, you may have rights to obtain information about processing, access or correct your personal information, request erasure, withdraw consent, raise a grievance, or use other rights available to you. A workspace administrator can help with organizational records within that administrator&#x27;s authority. Applicable rights are not limited to the application controls currently available.</p><p>Use Settings → Account → Sign-in &amp; Security to submit an authenticated full-export, account-closure/deletion or erasure-review request, or contact contact@planebyte.com. The application acknowledges the request with a reference. Support reviews identity, workspace authority and retention exceptions, requests proportionate clarification where needed, and records verified closure and the applicable 30/90-day targets. Use the case reference with the same contact for status, correction or escalation. Request handling also observes any additional applicable legal requirements. No named privacy officer or legally required designation is invented.</p><p>Use the approved channel and provide enough context to locate the relevant account/workspace without sending a password, OTP or unnecessary identity document. Verification should be proportionate and prevent one person from deleting another person&#x27;s or organization&#x27;s records. We will explain any valid reason a request cannot be completed in full and any available escalation or review route under the approved process and applicable law.</p><p>Deletion or withdrawal may affect features that need the relevant information. Retention may remain necessary for payment/refund and legal records, fraud/security investigations, dispute resolution, lawful holds, contact history or suppression choices. No blanket exception permits keeping every record indefinitely, and no request automatically authorizes a production reset or deletion of another workspace. Closing access is not a substitute for implementing and verifying an approved deletion request.</p></section><section id="privacy-10"><h2>11. Security and incidents</h2><p>The service uses workspace authorization, protected authentication/session records, server-side secret handling, access controls and protected storage/transit mechanisms appropriate to the relevant component. No system can guarantee absolute security. We do not claim a security certification or guarantee against every incident.</p><p>We investigate suspected incidents, take appropriate containment and recovery measures, and provide notifications required by applicable law and the relevant agreement. Contact contact@planebyte.com if you suspect unauthorized access, and avoid disclosing another person&#x27;s information or credentials in the report. Support, privacy and security escalation use contact@planebyte.com, addressed to the business owner, Piyush Agrawal.</p></section><section id="privacy-11"><h2>12. Eligibility, policy changes and contact</h2><p>The service is intended for authorized business use. It is not designed to solicit children&#x27;s personal information. If information appears to have been supplied without the authority or lawful consent required for that person, contact us so it can be assessed through the approved request process. No unapproved age threshold or guardian-consent mechanism is asserted by this draft.</p><p>The published Policy will identify its version and effective date. Material changes will be communicated as required, and additional consent will be obtained where the applicable processing requires it. A policy update does not silently authorize unrelated outreach, a payment or a new source connection.</p><p>Support and privacy contact: contact@planebyte.com, addressed to Piyush Agrawal, business owner. Responsible business: Planebyte (Individual), House No. 1005, Attabira, Bargarh, Odisha 768027, India. Use the request and escalation process in section 10. Mandatory rights and lawful access to an appropriate authority remain available.</p></section></article></section></main>--- a/src/markup/cancellation-refund-policy.html
